WEBVTT
NOTE how-does-thermohaline-circulation-work - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.763
Water sinking in the North Atlantic may
not see sunlight for about 1,000 years.

00:00:04.763 --> 00:00:09.500
That is NOAA's estimate for how long a
single parcel of seawater takes to finish

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.408
one lap of the deep current often called
the global conveyor belt.

00:00:13.408 --> 00:00:17.973
Thermohaline circulation is a slow,
planet-wide overturning of the ocean

00:00:17.973 --> 00:00:23.109
driven by differences in seawater density,
which depends on temperature and salt.

00:00:23.109 --> 00:00:24.509
That part is textbook.

00:00:24.509 --> 00:00:29.216
What the textbook picture tends to hide is
the harder half of the problem.

00:00:29.216 --> 00:00:31.554
Making water sink is easy to explain.

00:00:31.554 --> 00:00:36.674
Bringing all that cold, heavy water back
up to the surface is not, and the answer

00:00:36.674 --> 00:00:40.592
turned out to involve forces the word
thermohaline leaves out.

00:00:40.592 --> 00:00:42.997
How does thermohaline circulation work?

00:00:42.997 --> 00:00:45.199
The word itself is the first clue.

00:00:45.199 --> 00:00:50.572
Thermo means heat and haline means salt,
so thermohaline circulation is circulation

00:00:50.572 --> 00:00:52.968
driven by heat and salt, not by wind.

00:00:52.968 --> 00:00:58.083
Both change how dense seawater is. Colder
water is denser than warmer water.

00:00:58.083 --> 00:01:00.736
Saltier water is denser than fresher
water.

00:01:00.736 --> 00:01:05.795
Where water at the surface becomes denser
than the water beneath it, it sinks, and

00:01:05.795 --> 00:01:09.373
it keeps sinking until it meets water of
matching density.

00:01:09.373 --> 00:01:14.499
That is the whole engine in a sentence,
and it is worth noticing how it is known.

00:01:14.499 --> 00:01:18.092
Density cannot be seen from a ship. It is
calculated.

00:01:18.092 --> 00:01:23.178
Oceanographers lower instruments that
measure conductivity, temperature and

00:01:23.178 --> 00:01:26.568
depth, and from conductivity they derive
salinity.

00:01:26.568 --> 00:01:31.424
Feed temperature, salinity and pressure
into the equation of state for seawater,

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:32.760
and density comes out.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:38.156
Map that density across an ocean basin,
and the layers appear: light water on top,

00:01:38.156 --> 00:01:41.907
heavy water stacked below, sloping
surfaces between them.

00:01:41.907 --> 00:01:46.177
The circulation was inferred from that
layering long before anyone measured a

00:01:46.177 --> 00:01:47.397
deep current directly.

00:01:47.397 --> 00:01:52.155
If cold, salty water sinks, you might
expect it to sink everywhere at once near

00:01:52.155 --> 00:01:52.757
the poles.

00:01:52.757 --> 00:01:58.267
It does not. Deep water forms in a few
polar regions, and the reasons are

00:01:58.267 --> 00:02:03.213
In the far North Atlantic, warm salty
water carried north by surface currents

00:02:03.213 --> 00:02:05.589
gives up its heat to cold winter air.

00:02:05.589 --> 00:02:11.227
It keeps its salt, so it becomes both cold
and salty, and heavy enough to sink.

00:02:11.227 --> 00:02:16.485
That water spreads south at depth as what
oceanographers call North Atlantic Deep

00:02:16.485 --> 00:02:16.875
Water.

00:02:16.875 --> 00:02:20.186
Around Antarctica, a second process does
the work.

00:02:20.186 --> 00:02:24.027
When seawater freezes, the ice that forms
is mostly fresh.

00:02:24.027 --> 00:02:29.519
Much of the salt is pushed out into the
water left behind, a process called brine

00:02:29.519 --> 00:02:30.197
rejection.

00:02:30.197 --> 00:02:35.149
That leftover water is extremely cold and
extra salty, and it becomes Antarctic

00:02:35.149 --> 00:02:40.163
Bottom Water, the densest water in the
open ocean, which hugs the seafloor as it

00:02:40.163 --> 00:02:41.040
spreads north.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.805
Between them, these two sources fill most
of the deep ocean.

00:02:44.805 --> 00:02:49.887
The North Pacific, by contrast, is too
fresh at the surface to form deep water of

00:02:49.887 --> 00:02:50.389
its own.

00:02:50.389 --> 00:02:55.334
The evidence for where sinking happens
comes from looking in winter, exactly when

00:02:55.334 --> 00:02:57.104
ships least want to be there.

00:02:57.104 --> 00:03:01.098
Research ships measure winter columns of
water in the Labrador Sea and the seas

00:03:01.098 --> 00:03:05.092
around Greenland, and they have found the
surface layer stirred down deep, with

00:03:05.092 --> 00:03:08.075
temperature and salinity nearly uniform
from top to bottom.

00:03:08.075 --> 00:03:12.773
Floats record deep mixing in the same
places. Then the water can be followed.

00:03:12.773 --> 00:03:17.726
A tongue of slightly saltier, oxygen-rich
water extends south through the deep

00:03:17.726 --> 00:03:22.361
Atlantic, visible in density sections
taken across the basin decade after

00:03:22.361 --> 00:03:22.805
decade.

00:03:22.805 --> 00:03:26.789
The dense water spreads at depth, and its
fingerprint travels with it.

00:03:26.789 --> 00:03:31.134
Here is the problem that took
oceanographers decades to state clearly.

00:03:31.134 --> 00:03:35.788
If water only ever sank, the deep ocean
would slowly fill with the coldest,

00:03:35.788 --> 00:03:38.581
densest water available and then stop
moving.

00:03:38.581 --> 00:03:39.686
It does not stop.

00:03:39.686 --> 00:03:44.883
So something must be lifting deep water
back toward the surface, against its own

00:03:44.883 --> 00:03:46.832
weight, and that takes energy.

00:03:46.832 --> 00:03:49.190
Heat and salt alone cannot supply it.

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:53.970
The oceanographer Walter Munk worked
through the arithmetic and argued that

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:59.197
slow turbulent mixing, stirred by tides
and by internal waves breaking deep inside

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:04.487
the ocean, could gradually blend cold deep
water with warmer water above and let it

00:04:04.487 --> 00:04:04.805
rise.

00:04:04.805 --> 00:04:07.524
It was a careful argument, and it set up a
test.

00:04:07.524 --> 00:04:12.170
When researchers later released a harmless
tracer into the open ocean interior and

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:16.418
watched it spread, the mixing they
measured was weaker than his calculation

00:04:16.418 --> 00:04:16.928
required.

00:04:16.928 --> 00:04:22.062
Mixing turned out to be patchy: weak in
the quiet interior, much stronger over

00:04:22.062 --> 00:04:25.616
rough seafloor such as mid-ocean ridges
and seamounts.

00:04:25.616 --> 00:04:28.591
The other part of the answer lies around
Antarctica.

00:04:28.591 --> 00:04:33.340
Strong westerly winds circle the continent
with no land to stop them, and they push

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:35.171
surface water away to the north.

00:04:35.171 --> 00:04:39.638
Water from below rises to replace it,
climbing along density layers that slope

00:04:39.638 --> 00:04:42.387
upward toward the surface in the Southern
Ocean.

00:04:42.387 --> 00:04:46.801
Much of the deep water that sank in the
North Atlantic is thought to return this

00:04:46.801 --> 00:04:47.021
way.

00:04:47.021 --> 00:04:51.811
So the full route runs like this: water
sinks near the poles, spreads through the

00:04:51.811 --> 00:04:56.718
deep basins, rises in the Southern Ocean
and elsewhere, and is warmed and freshened

00:04:56.718 --> 00:04:57.960
again at the surface.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.020
Winds and mixing supply the lift.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:04.640
That is why many oceanographers now prefer
the name meridional overturning

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:05.389
circulation.

00:05:05.389 --> 00:05:10.149
It describes what the water does without
claiming that heat and salt alone are

00:05:10.149 --> 00:05:10.699
doing it.

00:05:10.699 --> 00:05:12.891
Knowledge is made, not announced.

00:05:12.891 --> 00:05:18.072
It comes from observation, measurement,
argument, failed attempts, correction,

00:05:18.072 --> 00:05:22.988
replication — and people willing to
separate what they know from what they

00:05:22.988 --> 00:05:23.984
merely suspect.

00:05:23.984 --> 00:05:28.497
Much of what we know about the deep route
was read from chemistry rather than

00:05:28.497 --> 00:05:30.197
measured with current meters.

00:05:30.197 --> 00:05:35.479
Surface water takes up carbon-14 from the
atmosphere, the same radioactive form of

00:05:35.479 --> 00:05:37.669
carbon used to date wood and bone.

00:05:37.669 --> 00:05:40.170
Once that water sinks, it is cut off.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:45.510
There is no new carbon-14 after sinking,
and what the water carries decays at a

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:46.795
steady, known rate.

00:05:46.795 --> 00:05:48.974
So less carbon-14 means older water.

00:05:48.974 --> 00:05:53.697
When survey ships sampled the world's
oceans basin by basin, the pattern lined

00:05:53.697 --> 00:05:58.843
up with the conveyor picture: younger deep
water in the North Atlantic, progressively

00:05:58.843 --> 00:06:03.202
older water along the route, and the
oldest water lies in the deep North

00:06:03.202 --> 00:06:05.381
Pacific, at the far end of the path.

00:06:05.381 --> 00:06:08.105
Other tracers tell the short end of the
story.

00:06:08.105 --> 00:06:12.663
Chlorofluorocarbons, industrial gases that
only entered the atmosphere in the

00:06:12.663 --> 00:06:17.577
twentieth century, turn up in deep North
Atlantic water, which shows that water was

00:06:17.577 --> 00:06:18.997
at the surface recently.

00:06:18.997 --> 00:06:23.408
Dissolved oxygen falls steadily along the
route too, used up by living things with

00:06:23.408 --> 00:06:24.645
no way to replenish it.

00:06:24.645 --> 00:06:28.736
For most of the twentieth century, the
deep circulation was a deduction.

00:06:28.736 --> 00:06:33.808
Measuring it directly is hard, because the
flow is slow and the ocean is enormous.

00:06:33.808 --> 00:06:36.016
Two kinds of instrument changed that.

00:06:36.016 --> 00:06:40.553
The first is the deep mooring: a cable
anchored to the seafloor, strung with

00:06:40.553 --> 00:06:45.507
current meters and sensors that record for
months or years before a ship returns to

00:06:45.507 --> 00:06:46.283
collect them.

00:06:46.283 --> 00:06:50.295
Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution is
among the groups that have run deep

00:06:50.295 --> 00:06:51.632
moorings in the Atlantic.

00:06:51.632 --> 00:06:57.280
The second is the Argo program, a global
fleet of robotic Argo floats, with Scripps

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:02.656
playing a leading role, which drift below
the surface, sink and rise to measure

00:07:02.656 --> 00:07:06.059
temperature and salinity, and report by
satellite.

00:07:06.059 --> 00:07:10.845
Lines of moorings strung across the
Atlantic now give continuous estimates of

00:07:10.845 --> 00:07:12.709
how much water is overturning.

00:07:12.709 --> 00:07:18.346
NOAA's public explainer of the global
conveyor belt summarises what this kind of

00:07:18.346 --> 00:07:19.403
evidence shows.

00:07:19.403 --> 00:07:23.146
Those records confirmed some things and
complicated others.

00:07:23.146 --> 00:07:26.192
The overturning is real, and it can be
measured.

00:07:26.192 --> 00:07:31.006
But it varies from year to year, and even
from month to month, by more than anyone

00:07:31.006 --> 00:07:33.472
expected from the smooth conveyor diagram.

00:07:33.472 --> 00:07:38.907
That variability is the reason a settled
long-term trend has been hard to pin down.

00:07:38.907 --> 00:07:43.618
A record that swings widely needs to run
for a long time before a slow change can

00:07:43.618 --> 00:07:45.013
be separated from noise.

00:07:45.013 --> 00:07:50.810
And because most floats sample only the
upper ocean, how the deepest layers change

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:52.507
remains thinly observed.

00:07:52.507 --> 00:07:57.076
Deep-capable floats are being added, but
the abyss is still the least sampled part

00:07:57.076 --> 00:07:57.856
of the system.

00:07:57.856 --> 00:08:02.554
One of the most important ideas about this
system came from a deliberately crude

00:08:02.554 --> 00:08:02.907
model.

00:08:02.907 --> 00:08:08.540
Henry Stommel represented the ocean as two
connected boxes of water, one warm and one

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:12.715
cold, and let heat and salt compete to
drive flow between them.

00:08:12.715 --> 00:08:16.122
Temperature differences pushed the
circulation one way.

00:08:16.122 --> 00:08:18.661
Salt differences could push it the other.

00:08:18.661 --> 00:08:22.853
The striking result was that the model
could settle into two stable states:

00:08:22.853 --> 00:08:26.485
strong overturning or weak overturning
under the same conditions.

00:08:26.485 --> 00:08:32.133
Which one it chose depended on its
history. That is a model result, not a

00:08:32.133 --> 00:08:37.276
But it suggested that adding enough fresh
water at high latitudes, from rain, rivers

00:08:37.276 --> 00:08:42.051
or melting ice, could make surface water
too light to sink and push the system

00:08:42.051 --> 00:08:43.520
toward its weaker state.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:47.323
Evidence from the past suggests this is
more than a thought experiment.

00:08:47.323 --> 00:08:52.073
Ocean sediment cores, layered mud drilled
from the seafloor, preserve chemical

00:08:52.073 --> 00:08:57.007
signals of past deep water, and some of
those records line up with abrupt climate

00:08:57.007 --> 00:08:58.773
shifts seen in Greenland ice.

00:08:58.773 --> 00:09:03.660
Many researchers interpret those episodes
as times when the Atlantic overturning

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:04.699
weakened sharply.

00:09:04.699 --> 00:09:09.505
Climate models generally project weakening
this century as the high latitudes warm

00:09:09.505 --> 00:09:10.208
and freshen.

00:09:10.208 --> 00:09:15.307
How much, and whether any threshold lies
within reach, remains actively debated,

00:09:15.307 --> 00:09:19.195
and the direct measurements are still too
short to settle it.

00:09:19.195 --> 00:09:23.014
When you see this circulation animated,
notice the speed.

00:09:23.014 --> 00:09:28.509
A glowing ribbon looping the globe in a
few seconds is a speeded-up summary, not a

00:09:28.509 --> 00:09:29.515
real-time view.

00:09:29.515 --> 00:09:35.448
Real deep flow is slow and broad, more
like a whole basin creeping than a river

00:09:35.448 --> 00:09:36.424
in a channel.

00:09:36.424 --> 00:09:40.440
Notice too that the ribbon is drawn as one
continuous line.

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:45.613
In the water, the route branches,
recirculates in eddies and mixes along the

00:09:45.613 --> 00:09:49.016
way, so no single parcel follows the path
you see.

00:09:49.016 --> 00:09:51.011
And watch where the arrows rise.

00:09:51.011 --> 00:09:56.187
If deep water rises with no wind or mixing
shown to lift it, the drawing is simpler

00:09:56.187 --> 00:09:57.309
than the evidence.

00:09:57.309 --> 00:10:00.263
Here is what all of this does not
establish.

00:10:00.263 --> 00:10:05.297
The figure of about 1,000 years is an
estimate for a typical circuit, not a

00:10:05.297 --> 00:10:10.802
clock carried by any real parcel of water;
some water returns far sooner, and some

00:10:10.802 --> 00:10:12.077
lingers far longer.

00:10:12.077 --> 00:10:17.192
Radiocarbon gives an age, not a route, so
the path between the North Atlantic and

00:10:17.192 --> 00:10:19.528
the North Pacific is partly inferred.

00:10:19.528 --> 00:10:23.791
The relative share of lift supplied by
Southern Ocean winds versus deep mixing is

00:10:23.791 --> 00:10:24.739
still argued over.

00:10:24.739 --> 00:10:28.670
And the instrument record of the
overturning is only a couple of decades

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:31.837
long, too short to say with confidence how
it is changing.

00:10:31.837 --> 00:10:37.263
The ocean's deepest water is moving now,
slowly, on a schedule longer than any

00:10:37.263 --> 00:10:38.307
record we hold.

00:10:38.307 --> 00:10:42.852
We know its shape from chemistry, from
moorings and floats, and from the honest

00:10:42.852 --> 00:10:46.764
gap between what the water should do and
what it was measured doing.

00:10:46.764 --> 00:10:49.928
That gap is where the next lap of
understanding begins.
